Fillets: the rounding of an edge that another rounding rolls on all along
is dropped (mitcad#1500).

A roof slab's eave, a level face 1.506 wide between an acute edge (60
degrees, at the tip) and an obtuse one (120 degrees), both rounded with
0.9 or 1: the tip's ball touches the eave 1.73 r from the tip, past the
obtuse edge from r = 0.87, so the tip's rounding rolls on the obtuse edge
(0011, RollsInsteadOfOverflow) and consumes the eave. The obtuse edge's
own rounding, on the eave and the underside, was computed as well, its
contact on the eave inside the face the other consumes; the corners at its
ends failed ("OneCorner : fillets have too big radiuses").

Below r = 0.87 the two roundings overlap on the eave and the obtuse edge's
one rides on the tip's contact line there (0016): its ball touches the
underside and passes through that line. Where the tip's blend rolls on the
obtuse edge, that line is the edge itself, and the riding ball touches the
underside on the edge: a section of no width, the rounding takes nothing
away (as the ride's own does as r grows to 0.87).

- Compute, after DropOverlapsRunOutByOther: an open stripe of one edge
  that another stripe of one SurfData rolls on from vertex to vertex (a
  side on that edge as curve, rollsOnWholeEdge), the two edges of a kind
  (both convex or both concave) and bounding one face, is dropped with its
  corners, as RemoveCoincidentStripes drops a stripe (it stays a contour of
  the builder and gives no faces).

